#!/usr/bin/env python3
# tool: redpen. Serve any HTML with a comment overlay; Nigel marks it up and the comments come back to the agent as structured data. `redpen <dir> --port 8801` then `show 8801 --path /x.html`. Was called `annotate`.
"""Serve a directory with an annotation overlay, and collect comments as files.

    redpen <dir> [--port 8801]

Why this exists: an agent builds a page and serves it; you look at it and want to
say "this heading is wrong" about a specific thing. Screenshots into a bug tracker
are the usual answer, but an agent does not want a picture. It wants the node.
So an annotation here carries the selected text, a CSS selector, the element's own
HTML and its position, and the agent goes straight to it.

How it fits together:

    agent (server)          redpen <dir> --port 8801        serves + collects
    you (laptop)            peek 8765                      tunnels + opens
    you (browser)           select text, or drag a box, then comment
    agent (server)          reads <dir>/.annotations/*.json

Binds 127.0.0.1 by default: the page material and your comments stay on the machine
serving them, which is the machine the agent is on, so nothing is copied back.

Interactions:
  - Select text, then press A (or right-click) to comment on the selection.
  - Hold Shift and drag to lasso a region, then comment on what it covers.
  - Esc cancels.
"""

